// fuzz-mutate-10: let-inline moves a value that raises (`x?`, or a call
// whose callee raises) under a later `catch`, so the raise reaches that
// handler instead of the one it had: a false let-inline typeflip
// command: graphix-fuzz typemorph design/review-2026-10-05/repro/fuzz-mutate-10.gx
// (base: graphix --check accepts, warning that the raise is not caught)
// expected: typemorph: no flips. let-inline's only catch guard is the
// reverse case, a value holding a `catch` moved into a seq body
// (typemorph.rs:270-276).
// observed: exit 1,
// TYPEFLIP fuzz-mutate-10.gx let-inline#1: N: type mismatch Error<`A> does not
// contain Error<ErrChain<`B>>
//   mutant: { let x: [i64, Error<`B>] = 1; let k = 2; let r = { catch(e: Error<`A>) 0; (x?) + k }; r }
// The refusal is the language rule (a catch installs its handler for the
// rest of its block): `graphix --check` on the mutant refuses it at the
// catch. The same flip with `let f = |x: [i64, Error<`B>]| x?; .. let v =
// f(x)` in place of `let v = x?` (let-inline#2, mutant `.. (f(x)) + k ..`).
{ let x: [i64, Error<`B>] = 1; let v = x?; let k = 2; let r = { catch(e: Error<`A>) 0; v + k }; r }
